# Line endings are pinned here, not left to each machine's git config.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in its system gitconfig, which
# rewrites checkouts to CRLF. Every blob in this repo is LF, so that setting
# made `git add` warn on every file it touched — and, worse, the artefact is a
# static-musl binary in a distroless Linux container, where a CRLF line in a
# shell script or a Dockerfile is a runtime failure rather than a cosmetic
# difference. `eol=lf` overrides `core.autocrlf` for this working tree, so the
# behaviour no longer depends on who cloned it or on which platform.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binaries; the explicit entries below are
# for the formats present in the tree, so detection is never the thing standing
# between a PNG and a corrupted checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary

# PowerShell is the one deliberate exception: `.ps1` files run on Windows,
# where CRLF is the native convention and some older hosts mis-parse a lone LF.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
